    <p class="eyebrow">THE SACRED PLACES JOURNAL</p><h1>A river. An island.<br><em>A living heritage.</em></h1>
    <p>Places hold stories in a different way: through rivers, shrines and the communities that care for them. Begin with two centres of pilgrimage and follow their connections into the collection.</p>
    <nav class="journal-index" aria-label="Place directory"><a href="#varanasi">Varanasi · Uttar Pradesh</a><a href="#rameswaram">Rameswaram · Tamil Nadu</a></nav>
  </section>
  <div class="section heritage-dossiers">
    <article class="reader-panel heritage-dossier" id="varanasi">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 / ON THE GANGA</p><h2>Varanasi</h2>
      <dl class="journal-facts"><div><dt>Also known as</dt><dd>Kashi and Banaras</dd></div><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>Uttar Pradesh, northern India, on the River Ganga</dd></div><div><dt>A shrine to explore</dt><dd>Shri Kashi Vishwanath, dedicated to Shiva and revered as a jyotirlinga shrine</dd></div></dl>
      <p>Varanasi’s riverfront brings pilgrimage, worship and everyday life into close proximity. Ghats form the city’s meeting places with the Ganga; narrow lanes lead into its temple landscape.</p>
      <h3>Shiva and the river</h3>
      <p>The Kashi Vishwanath shrine offers a connection to Shiva’s devotional world. The Ganga offers another doorway into the stories: in the Ramayana’s account of Bhagiratha, Shiva receives the river’s descent before she follows the king onward.</p>
      <p>This is a reading connection between a sacred river and a narrative in the collection. The Bhagiratha preview describes the river’s descent, rather than a historical account of Varanasi.</p>
      <div class="journal-links"><a href="/deities.html#shiva">Explore Shiva</a><a href="/stories.html#story-bhagiratha-ganga">Read the Bhagiratha and Ganga preview</a><a href="/festivals.html#shivaratri">Explore Maha Shivaratri</a></div>
